From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 06:23:11 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] drm/asahi: mmu: retain a VM's page tables when its
 root lease was taken over

A VM's page tables are freed only when the whole tree drops, and the
application context's root lease is returned before its VM: the return
clears the TTBAT slot and invalidates the context's ASID, so no walk of the
tree can remain cached in the GPU MMU when its table pages go back to the
page allocator. The firmware's device tree asks for that guarantee
explicitly (issue-gmmu-tlbis-at-retype).

When the return finds another root in the slot, it must not clear the slot
or invalidate on that owner's behalf. It only logged that case, and the
VM's tables were then still freed with nothing having invalidated their
walks.

Classify the slot at return as owned, already cleared, or taken over
(mmu/release.rs, core-only). A taken-over lease marks itself, and the
execution context retains the VM's page tables for good, on its explicit
release and on drop, before its VM can drop. The owned and cleared cases
behave exactly as before; the host test checks that the new classification
invalidates in every case the old rule did, and that a taken-over lease
leads to no free_table call.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
---
